Tool use has its own sub-metrics because most agent failures are tool failures in disguise.

**Tool description quality**: Before measuring the agent, measure whether your tool descriptions are even legible. Run a probe: give the LLM only the tool's docstring and ask "When would you use this?" If it can't explain, your description is the bug, not the model.

**Per-tool precision and recall**:

- **Tool precision**: Of the times the agent picked tool X, how often was X the correct choice?
- **Tool recall**: Of the times tool X was the correct choice, how often did the agent pick it?

These reveal *which* tools confuse the agent. A 95% TSA can hide the fact that recall on `database_query` is 40%.

**Argument extraction F1**: Treat each argument as a token to be extracted. Compute F1 over (key, value) pairs, just like NER. This is what ToolBench-style benchmarks report.

**Tool execution success (TES)**: Did the API call actually return 200? This is *environment* quality, not agent quality, but if TES is 60%, no agent can succeed. Always measure it as a sanity check on the environment.

```python
def per_tool_pr(pred_actions, gold_actions):
    tools = set(a["tool"] for a in gold_actions)
    out = {}
    for t in tools:
        tp = sum(1 for p, g in zip(pred_actions, gold_actions)
                 if p["tool"] == t and g["tool"] == t)
        fp = sum(1 for p, g in zip(pred_actions, gold_actions)
                 if p["tool"] == t and g["tool"] != t)
        fn = sum(1 for p, g in zip(pred_actions, gold_actions)
                 if p["tool"] != t and g["tool"] == t)
        prec = tp / (tp + fp) if (tp + fp) else 0.0
        rec  = tp / (tp + fn) if (tp + fn) else 0.0
        out[t] = {"precision": prec, "recall": rec}
    return out
```

**ToolBench / ToolLLM (brief)**: ToolBench is a large-scale benchmark built on RapidAPI — 16k+ real APIs across 49 categories. The agent must read API docs, pick the right API, fill in arguments, and chain calls. It introduced **Pass-rate** (did the call execute?) and **Win-rate** (human preference over trajectory pairs, proxied by a GPT-4 judge). ToolLLM is the model fine-tuned on this data. The benchmark's core lesson: tool diversity breaks naive function-calling — argument hallucination is the dominant failure mode.
